WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.100
Film Courage: I’m not sure if you’re okay
talking about this but did you go through

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:07.879
a period of time where you were not able to
pay your bills being a cinematographer?

00:00:07.879 --> 00:00:12.860
Andy Rydzewski, Cinematographer: I’m happy
to discuss that, yes.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:23.220
I’ve been in Los Angeles for 15 years (something
like that) and I’ve been broke for probably

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.830
11 of them, 10…something like that?

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:33.230
Problematically broke for five, six years
and I feel very lucky.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:38.521
This is one of the things that I am always
very thankful for is that I always knew that

00:00:38.521 --> 00:00:43.710
I wanted to be involved in filmmaking or at
least from a very young age.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.230
I didn’t know if it was realistic.

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:46.690
My mother wanted me to be a doctor.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:51.050
My whole family are doctors except for me.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:01:00.310
But I just loved it so much…filmmaking…the
idea of it and any little taste I got.

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:04.410
I slept in my car…my last semester of undergrad
I slept in my car.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.070
I went to the gym in the morning to shower.

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:09.560
I never felt…it kind of felt romantic.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:18.600
I never felt like I was sacrificing even though
I guess I was, it didn’t feel that way because

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:26.780
I was pursuing this kind of weird dream and
it always and money…I will say it’s tricky

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:34.659
if you’re raising a family and all of that
but for me it has always served me well to

00:01:34.659 --> 00:01:39.481
ignore money in the jobs I pursue and all
of that.

00:01:39.481 --> 00:01:45.479
I have friends who are doing well in let’s
say the commercial world or the corporate

00:01:45.479 --> 00:01:53.399
world in terms of filmmaking and they will
tell me that they are envious of me because

00:01:53.399 --> 00:02:00.240
I’ve shot some features and TV shows but
then something will come their way some low-budget

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.820
feature and they will say no because of the
pay is no good.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:09.840
But then they get frustrated that they are
not getting a feature but they just want…and

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.939
narrative pays worse than commercials.

00:02:12.939 --> 00:02:20.189
That is generally the rule and for me ignoring
the money and just chasing the project or

00:02:20.189 --> 00:02:23.650
the director has served me very well.

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:27.700
Some of the best, some of the jobs you know
I think about the last show I shot which was

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:33.879
PEN15 a Hulu series and that director worked
together on multiple projects.

00:02:33.879 --> 00:02:43.220
We shot a series before for Seeso which is
a defunct streaming service and we shot a

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:48.190
web series for I think it was three days or
four days of shooting.

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:53.780
I think I got paid $100.00 and I worked my
tail off and still to this day it’s one

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:56.010
of my favorite things that I shot.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:01.129
I believe for him he got his manager from
that.

00:03:01.129 --> 00:03:04.900
He had a screening, it went very well.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:11.120
It kind of kicked things off for him and at
the time when he asked me to do it I did hesitate.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:12.930
I was like “Well…okay.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:13.930
It barely pays.”

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:17.379
Like it’s barely paying me anything and
then I did it and I loved it!

00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:18.530
I loved the project.

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:20.540
I love everybody involved.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:26.489
A lot of those people are doing well these
days and it’s a piece of my community in

00:03:26.489 --> 00:03:33.340
Los Angeles came from that tiny webseries
so for me…and we will see now.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:41.790
We are in a pandemic as we speak and I haven’t
worked in months and I was okay with it for

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:48.930
a time and now the anxiety is starting to
creep in but you know I’ve been freelance

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:55.120
for 15 years and I am used to all manner of
ups and downs and I keep my overhead low so

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.999
that I don’t have to chase money I guess…yes.

00:03:58.999 --> 00:04:05.719
Film Courage: Were you actively looking for
jobs or because so much of the film industry

00:04:05.719 --> 00:04:07.560
just completely shut down…?

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:19.289
Andy: So yes now at this point the only thing
that I have done to be proactive really is

00:04:19.289 --> 00:04:26.210
I am not really active with social media and
I realize that for my job I probably should,

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:31.500
I should make that a higher priority and I
do have friends who are really good with Instagram

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:40.880
specifically and I realize that it has the
potential to be a good tool for finding work

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:48.930
and so I am now trying to treat it a little
more seriously and trying to create posts

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:51.590
that I would like to see.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:56.930
Maybe a little educational or a behind the
scenes or how did you light this or why is

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:58.780
the color palette this?

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:05.520
That kind of thing so I am trying to now just
so I feel proactive because I don’t really

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.230
know what else I can be doing to find work.

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:13.840
I’ve built a decent network over the years.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:24.199
I have a reputation that I am proud of but
when people aren’t calling I’m genuinely

00:05:24.199 --> 00:05:27.960
not sure of what one does and I am learning.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.199
I’m trying to figure it out.

00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:36.340
I’m talking to different people and seeing
what they think and just kind of trusting

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:39.840
that something will come.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:47.400
The last show I shot did well in terms of
its reviews and finding an audience so I am

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:55.160
hoping that that might put my name on some
lists but the truth is so much of this is

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:56.289
a crapshoot.

00:05:56.289 --> 00:06:02.599
I really don’t know how to find work even
when a decade ago I woke up every day and

00:06:02.599 --> 00:06:09.790
I would start on Craigslist and Mandy (dot)
com was a thing but I was trying to do that

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:15.199
the other day I was like curious if people
were posting jobs during all of this.

00:06:15.199 --> 00:06:19.730
But I have never been sure how to find work.

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:22.740
It’s never made sense to me.

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:29.509
But I have been okay at building relationships
and not in an aggressive networking way I

00:06:29.509 --> 00:06:30.650
don’t think.

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:36.780
It’s mostly I’m a big film nerd and I
end up bonding with people who are also film

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:44.090
nerds and the directors who I have bonded
with, it tends to be somebody who I met somewhere

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:48.160
and we just got to talking about some movie
that we saw and then it snowballs and you’re

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:50.260
like “Have you ever seen this movie?”

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:54.370
And just the excitement of it and I think
that is one of the arguments for being in

00:06:54.370 --> 00:07:03.669
Los Angeles is you are around other people
who are chasing the same dream and a lot of

00:07:03.669 --> 00:07:08.940
them you will find people who are nerdy in
the same way you’re nerdy and then you bond

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:16.900
and then going back to what I was saying earlier
about how you are on set that often starts

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:17.900
way before.

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:22.199
It could be a job interview but ideally it’s
a social setting.

00:07:22.199 --> 00:07:27.560
You are meeting a friend of a friend of a
friend at somebody’s birthday and you are

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:28.560
like “Oh?

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:29.720
I really liked that person.

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:31.389
What do they do?

00:07:31.389 --> 00:07:32.389
Oh…cinematographer.

00:07:32.389 --> 00:07:34.500
Well you know we have this thing coming up.”

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:41.889
I think that how a lot of Los Angeles connections
get made and I think that was the downside

00:07:41.889 --> 00:07:47.990
of going to such a small school is that I
came to LA with zero connections and I think

00:07:47.990 --> 00:07:54.770
that people who got to USC for instance which
is way more expensive so it has that downside

00:07:54.770 --> 00:08:01.419
but I think you are meeting people who are
going to be in the industry for the next decade

00:08:01.419 --> 00:08:06.110
at least and I think that gives you a real
head start in a lot of ways and now I don’t

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:10.480
know in terms of film education or the debt
and all of that.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:17.690
I don’t know the pros and cons but I think
yes meeting people which again in a pandemic

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:18.690
is tricky.

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:24.440
I have no idea how I am going to meet people
but again there are film groups that are meeting

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:31.740
on zoom and I should maybe try to be more
proactive about that and doing something like

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:32.740
this.

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:33.740
Who knows?

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:37.300
Who knows who will see the interview and be
like “Hey!

00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:38.300
That guy.”

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:39.300
Film Courage: “I like him!

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:42.140
I want to meditate in my car with him.”

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:43.459
Andy: Exactly, right!

00:08:43.459 --> 00:08:51.530
So you never know but I’ve never been good
at how to get work, how to summon work.

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:57.010
It’s definitely one of the, I’m fond of
saying this but I think it’s true, I am

00:08:57.010 --> 00:08:59.870
bad at the business side of the business.

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:04.220
Film Courage: I see a lot of true artists
are that way.

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:12.410
Andy: Yes, I have a lot of musician friends
and some of the best ones are terrible at

00:09:12.410 --> 00:09:19.890
every other part of life so yes, I don’t
know what one does with that but you just

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:27.230
kind of trust and I think the fact that I’ve
been freelance for so long and I’ve gone

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:35.880
through such lean times it prepares you for
future lean times.

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:37.760
So far I feel okay.

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:43.190
I think I told myself I’m allowed to get
anxious next March.

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:47.990
One year, at the one year anniversary if I
still have no work then I am allowed to start

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:49.050
getting a little anxious.

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:50.880
Film Courage: 2021?

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:52.100
Andy: 2021.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:53.910
I’m a little ahead of schedule.

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:58.170
I got anxious for the first time a week or
two ago, I’ve got to…that is not part

00:09:58.170 --> 00:10:03.300
of the deal so I’ve got to keep my…because
I know as soon as I end up on set and I am

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:06.150
on week four and I’m exhausted I’m going
to be like “Ahhh!

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:11.170
Remember when it was a pandemic and I could
just sleep whenever I wanted?”

00:10:11.170 --> 00:10:14.810
So I will be wishing for this downtime.

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:20.910
Film Courage: Well if we go back to 1918,
was it the Spanish Flu?

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:25.360
So was that two years if we look at the chart?

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:31.190
I’m not really sure but if we think about
medical advances are far greater now than

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:35.350
then and there is the whole thing does anyone
want to take a vaccine?

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:42.060
We won’t go down that slippery slope but
when you were talking about 2009 and The [Los

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:44.260
Angeles] Station Fire and all of that was
tragic.

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:50.029
We probably thought we’d never see anything
like that in our lifetime again and now you

00:10:50.029 --> 00:10:59.230
add a bad economy to a communicable disease
(sorry I can’t even say the word) so there

00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:03.920
are new variables that we’ve never really
been in this situation before.

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:09.000
Andy: I will say I think that is always on
the table.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:14.500
I think you know that life is change and as
soon as you think you have stability and a

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:18.890
predictability to your life I think something
is going to shake it up and that could be

00:11:18.890 --> 00:11:24.790
the fire speaking but that is how certainly
I try not to take anything for granted or

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:26.019
assume.

00:11:26.019 --> 00:11:32.490
I think also the film industry in and of itself
the number of projects that I was getting

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:36.550
ready to shoot that just fell through, I mean
it’s innumerable.

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:39.640
I was supposed to be shooting a feature this
summer that I was really excited about.

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:44.240
I had three features lined up this year and
poof you know.

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:54.040
But just in normal times that still happens
so I think being prepared for instability

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:59.060
in this industry is really important and weighing
if that is worth it.

00:11:59.060 --> 00:12:06.720
I’m lucky, I don’t have a wife and kids
and so my anxiety, any anxiety that arises

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:07.910
is just for me.

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:14.790
I would imagine that for somebody with a family
you have to provide for, that’s…I don’t

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:17.899
see how that couldn’t raise your stress.

00:12:17.899 --> 00:12:22.269
You know our industry is pretty cruel.

00:12:22.269 --> 00:12:23.269
It chews you up.

00:12:23.269 --> 00:12:24.269
It spits you out.

00:12:24.269 --> 00:12:29.610
There is always somebody behind you in line
so I don’t know.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:35.570
I think that is why you’ve really got to
love it because otherwise I do not recommend

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:40.310
getting into this industry and I’m lucky
because I do love it.

00:12:40.310 --> 00:12:44.190
I love it very purely so now we’ll see how
long that lasts.

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:49.360
I’m sure one morning I will wake up and
be like “Hmmm, I’m going to go see if

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:50.529
there is work at the gas station.”

00:12:50.529 --> 00:12:51.610
Who knows?

00:12:51.610 --> 00:12:55.930
Film Courage: Or you said you love ventriloquism
and things like that.

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:59.140
You never know, you might make a line of these
puppets?

00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:00.140
Andy: That’s true.

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:02.899
Film Courage: There are new things that can
happen and sometimes we don’t even know

00:13:02.899 --> 00:13:03.910
what are interests are.

00:13:03.910 --> 00:13:08.190
Andy: That’s true, you never know and again
I try to be open to all those things.

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:13.110
Life is change and it’s all an adventure
and tick-tock.

00:13:13.110 --> 00:13:15.279
Who knows how much time you’ve got left?

00:13:15.279 --> 00:13:24.150
I think being open to any other experiences,
any other paths that reveal themselves in

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:27.550
life like I don’t know, take it.

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:28.550
Why not?

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:29.680
What the heck.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:33.680
I’m always open to anything that might come
down the pike.

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:37.920
If I respond to it, that’s all I need and
then I will follow that.

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:43.449
Film Courage: When you look back at those
times when you were trying to find work and

00:13:43.449 --> 00:13:45.970
they were lean times, did you lose faith in
yourself?

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:49.630
Did you ever say “You know what, I should
have done pre-med?”

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:57.780
Andy: Hmm…I think in my leaner times there
were certainly doubts that came up like is

00:13:57.780 --> 00:13:58.780
this plausible.

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:05.450
I mean the truth is I still wonder can I do
this for another 10, 20, 30, 40 years and

00:14:05.450 --> 00:14:08.360
I genuinely don’t know.

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:21.160
So I think there have been doubts in terms
of is this a lifestyle that I want to continue

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:25.410
pursuing, that is probably the bigger one.

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:35.350
I had a year 2017 which was my first really
busy year and I shot…I can’t remember…it

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:42.940
was like 30, 40 episodes of half hour various
shows, half hour TV.

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:46.209
It was just I went from one show to the next
to the next.

00:14:46.209 --> 00:14:55.139
I spent six or seven months in other states
shooting and it was good obviously to be shooting.

00:14:55.139 --> 00:15:01.910
I was so exhausted by the end of the year
that I felt really burnt out and I got freaked

00:15:01.910 --> 00:15:02.910
out.

00:15:02.910 --> 00:15:04.029
I was like “Oh no.

00:15:04.029 --> 00:15:05.960
Can I not do this anymore?”

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:11.580
And it took me two months to recover just
sleeping basically for two months, I’ve

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:18.970
seen friends for the first time in a year
and it was the first time where I got scared

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:22.920
of what can happen in this industry on the
flip side.

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:29.160
I was used to being broke and not having work
and that brought its own anxiety and then

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:41.769
doing 13-hours days for a full year just ground
me down mentally and physically and so now

00:15:41.769 --> 00:15:49.529
when I have the luxury of choosing jobs I
now pay attention who am I working with?

00:15:49.529 --> 00:15:53.460
Is this really something that I feel a pull
toward?

00:15:53.460 --> 00:16:02.440
And trying to weigh pros and cons of everything
because I really don’t know.

00:16:02.440 --> 00:16:05.580
I really am scared of this industry.

00:16:05.580 --> 00:16:11.990
I’ve watched it take more talented people
and spit them out and turn them bitter and

00:16:11.990 --> 00:16:14.090
that’s something I am really worried about.

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:17.899
I don’t want that to be the case with me.

00:16:17.899 --> 00:16:22.050
I want to make sure that I still love what
I am doing for as long as possible.

00:16:22.050 --> 00:16:30.910
Part of that is meditation, going for evening
walks is like a huge thing.

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:33.160
It calms me down and all that.

00:16:33.160 --> 00:16:39.170
When I am working on set on a show, I have
a lot of strict rules.

00:16:39.170 --> 00:16:43.579
I have dietary rules and sleep rules that
I follow really closely because of that one

00:16:43.579 --> 00:16:44.910
year.

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:49.699
It was just so intense that it freaked me
out.

00:16:49.699 --> 00:16:57.120
So taking that experience and combining it
with years of being broke and having doubts

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:03.930
but always still just kind of okay, just keep
working on your craft and just hope I guess,

00:17:03.930 --> 00:17:05.800
hope that something works out.

00:17:05.800 --> 00:17:16.530
I don’t know, it’s all scary when I really
think about this industry it’s a tough one

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:18.650
to feel good about unfortunately.

00:17:18.650 --> 00:17:24.080
I’m hoping I mean who knows this pandemic
maybe I’m hoping it will turn into…I’ve

00:17:24.080 --> 00:17:29.330
heard some shows are switching to French hours
which means instead of a 12 and a half hour

00:17:29.330 --> 00:17:35.770
day with a half hour lunch in the middle is
you work for 10 hours straight, you don’t

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:39.990
stop for lunch, it’s a walking lunch and
I would love that!

00:17:39.990 --> 00:17:48.090
To go home 2 hours earlier is definitely worth
not sitting down for a half hour for lunch

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:49.400
but it helps your immune system.

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:56.010
We’re learning more about what lack of sleep
does to your body and it’s not good.

00:17:56.010 --> 00:18:04.080
So I do hope that be it a certain director
or a certain studio or whatever it is that

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:08.780
quality of life could maybe move up the list
in terms of priorities.

00:18:08.780 --> 00:18:10.180
That would be lovely.

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:16.040
I don’t have a lot of faith in that happening
but I do hope that there is a way to be doing

00:18:16.040 --> 00:18:21.790
this well into my 70’s and still feel like
I am taking care of my life.

00:18:21.790 --> 00:18:25.370
I don’t know if that is possible you know
but one can dream.

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:26.370
Question For The Viewers:

00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:26.380
Which parts of this video are most valuable
to you?

